.SH NAME
ssoossh-ssh-proxycommand - Ensure a valid certificate, then hand the connection to a relay command such as nc.


.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBssoossh ssh proxycommand <command> [args...] [flags]\fP


.SH DESCRIPTION
For use as ssh_config's ProxyCommand. Ensures a valid certificate is in your ssh-agent, then hands the connection to the relay command that follows it. Write the relay command exactly as you would without ssoossh:

.EX
Before: ProxyCommand /usr/bin/nc -X connect -x 192.0.2.0:8080 %h %p
After:  ProxyCommand ssoossh ssh proxycommand /usr/bin/nc -X connect -x 192.0.2.0:8080 %h %p
.EE

.PP
Everything from the relay command on is passed to it unparsed, so put ssoossh flags such as --server, --config or -v before it.

.PP
Requires an ssh-agent: ssh reads key files once, at startup, so a certificate written to disk afterwards would never be used. Without an agent, use the Match exec recipe from \fBssoossh ssh config\fR\&.


.SH OPTIONS
\fB-h\fP, \fB--help\fP[=false]
	help for proxycommand


.SH OPTIONS INHERITED FROM PARENT COMMANDS
\fB-c\fP, \fB--config\fP=""
	path to an extra config file, merged after the standard locations

.PP
\fB--server\fP=""
	server address, e.g. https://ssh.example.com; https is assumed when no scheme is given

.PP
\fB-v\fP, \fB--verbose\fP[=0]
	trace what the command is doing, to stderr; repeat for more (-v steps, -vv requests and files, -vvv bodies)


.SH SEE ALSO
